[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The Practice and the Dojo. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 6: Corey Haines from SCNA 2011 (2011).",
  "Corey was an itinerant software craftsman traveling between Chicago, Cleveland, and international dojos, demonstrating that code design could be practiced like a martial art.",
  "What inspired Corey Haines to give away Coderetreat for free to the global community? How did Kent Beck's Four Rules of Simple Design become a physical, embodied practice? What principles guided Corey Haines when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: rh9kzqKgKXA). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "When AI can generate an infinite volume of plausible code, constraint-based practice (no loops, no conditionals) is how humans retain structural judgment. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 7. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]